  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe expansion of the Flood Watch for Flash Flooding, in\ncollaboration with PBZ and ILN, fills in the greater southeast\nOhio area where the Slight Risk ERO exists. This fits the\nsynoptic pattern with the area of low pressure and its \nassociated warm front lifting northeast into the middle Ohio \nValley, and DESI rainfall exceedance probabilities.\n\n403 AM update...\nAn area of low pressure and its associated warm front will lift\nnortheast towards the region today, bringing a return of \nwidespread rain with embedded thunderstorms. Ahead of this, some\ndense fog is occurring across the mountains where low stratus \nis intersecting the higher terrain, with a Special Weather \nStatement currently in effect to account for this. Rain will \nquickly overspread the forecast area from south to north \nthroughout this morning, with periods of rain then expected \nthroughout the rest of the day into tonight amid cool \ntemperatures (mid 60s to low 70s across the lowlands) compared \nto late May normals. Rain could be heavy at times given a Gulf \nfeed, especially with any thunderstorm activity, which will be \nmost probable across northeast Kentucky, western West Virginia, \nand southeast Ohio. While severe weather is not anticipated, an \nisolated strong storm with gusty winds remains possible in these\nareas. Progged rainfall amounts through tonight generally range\nfrom less than half an inch (downsloping areas northwest of the\nhigher terrain), up to 1-2\" across northeast Kentucky, \nsoutheast Ohio, and western West Virginia. Given heavy rainfall \nacross this area over the last 48 hours, have issued a Flood \nWatch for flash flooding from 8 AM this morning until 8 AM \nSaturday morning. A slight expansion of the watch to include our\nfar northwest zones of Morgan/Perry counties will have to be \nmonitored.\n\nAdditional rounds of showers/storms are then expected \nthroughout the Memorial Day weekend, especially during the \nafternoon and evening hours, as the surface boundary and upper \nwaves continue to linger/impact the forecast area. Additional \nhydro concerns remain possible late Sunday into early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFor the short work week to follow, unsettled weather in the form\nof showers/storms is expected to continue on Tuesday and Wednesday\nas a surface boundary remains stationary across the area, with \nprecipitation most likely during the afternoon/evening hours. \nThe front may slip far enough southwest Thursday and especially \nFriday to allow for mainly dry conditions across the forecast \narea.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nExtensive cloud cover and rainfall today will keep temperatures\nbelow normal across the forecast area, with highs topping out in\nthe mid 60s to low 70s across the lowlands, while 50s to low 60s\nin the mountains. Temperatures warm to near or slightly above\nnormal on Saturday, with highs then progged to remain ~ 5\ndegrees above normal through much of next week.",
